 "drivingQuestion": "What novelty is in a one-day-old inheriting and bequeathing?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "Difficulty left for the next unit (Rav Sheshet's answer).",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "niddah.44a.17",
   "daf": "44a",
   "sentenceId": 16,
   "he": "נוֹחֵל וּמַנְחִיל. נוֹחֵל, מִמַּאן? מֵאָבִיו. וּמַנְחִיל, לְמַאן? לְאָחִיו מֵאָבִיו. אִי בָּעֵי מֵאֲבוּהּ (לֵירְתֵי) [לֵירְתוּ], וְאִי בָּעֵי מִינֵּיהּ (לֵירְתֵי) [לֵירְתוּ]!",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that this baby «inherits and he bequeaths.» The Gemara asks: «From whom does he inherit?» It must be «from his father. And to whom does he bequeath?» Presumably, he bequeaths «to his paternal brother,» in a case where the baby inherited his father’s property and then died on the same day, as maternal half-brothers do not inherit from each other. The Gemara raises a difficulty with regard to this interpretation: What is the novelty of the halakha that the brother of this one-day-old baby inherits from him? After all, «if» the surviving brother «wants, let him inherit from his father, and if he wants, let him inherit from» the one-day-old baby. Either way, he receives his late father’s property."
  }
 ],
